---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const tenure = assertion('lima-rents-and-the-townships-own')

const borrow = assertion('borrowing-against-a-house-in-lima')

const seven = assertion('the-denial-gap-is-seven-years-old')

const composition = assertion('the-lending-gap-is-not-composition')

const gap = assertion('the-denial-gap-that-does-not-resolve')

// `categories` and `values` are what the bar renderer reads. This helper was written with a
// line's `x` and `series` for a phase, so all five of this page's charts drew a trace over two
// undefined arrays and rendered empty — silently, because the table under each one was right
// and nothing typechecked the spec. See the check added to the gate.
const bars = (a: typeof tenure, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle },
  )

const borrowSpec = bars(borrow, 'Per cent of applications denied')

const gapSpec = bars(gap, 'Per cent of applications denied')

const sevenSpec = bars(seven, 'Per cent of applications denied')

const compositionSpec = bars(composition, 'Denials, 2018–2024')
---

<Article slug="buying-and-borrowing">
  <p>
    Every mortgage application acted on in this county is on public record, and seven years of it
    are read here: 24,269 decisions between 2018 and 2024. Lending ran to $513.1 million in 2020
    and $270.3 million in 2023, on between 175 and 209 institutions a year. One lender makes about
    a third of it — Superior Credit Union, of 540 W. Market Street in Lima, with 646 originations
    in 2023 where Rocket Mortgage made 63.
  </p>
  <p>
    This section was expected to be about buying a house in Lima, and the difference is not
    mostly there. An earlier version of this page said flatly that buying a house in Lima is no
    harder than buying one anywhere in the county. That was read off 2023 alone, which is the one
    year of seven in which Lima's purchase denial rate is the lower one; over the seven it is
    13.3 per cent against 10.8. The claim is withdrawn and the figures below replace it. What
    holds is the size of the two gaps: two and a half points on a purchase, sixteen on everything
    else.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={borrow} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="borrow"
    title="Denial rates by purpose, 2018–2024"
    caption="2018–2024, applications acted on — originated, approved and declined, or denied. 'In Lima' is the sixteen of the county's thirty-five census tracts whose population is majority Lima; they hold 32,575 of the city's 35,579 people and 3,093 who live outside it."
    columns={['Applications', 'Per cent denied']}
    rows={borrow.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="borrow" set:html={JSON.stringify(borrowSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The register's own reason moves with the geography. Twenty-one per cent of denials in Lima's
    tracts cite collateral, against 13 per cent elsewhere — 43 of 201 against 53 of 404 — where
    credit history leads in both. Collateral is the house, and the median house in Lima is worth
    $95,900 and was built in 1953. The median purchase loan originated in a Lima tract was
    $115,000; elsewhere in the county it was $175,000.
  </p>

  <h2>The difference that does not resolve</h2>
  <p>
    This one was published from a single year too, and seven years make it larger rather than
    smaller. The gap appears in all seven, between 5.9 and 17.0 percentage points, and never once
    reverses.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={seven} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="seven"
    title="Denial rate by applicant race, 2018–2024 pooled"
    caption="24,269 decisions across seven years. 1,334 applications record a Black applicant and 19,673 a white one; 18 per cent of all rows record no race and are in neither bar."
    columns={['Applicants', 'Per cent denied']}
    rows={seven.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="seven" set:html={JSON.stringify(sevenSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The obvious explanation is composition — that Black applicants in this county apply for
    different loans, at different incomes, with different debt and different equity. They do, and
    it is not most of the difference. Sorting every decision into a cell of loan purpose by income
    band by debt-to-income band by loan-to-value band gives 311 cells in which applications from
    both groups sit together.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={composition} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="composition"
    title="Denials among 955 applications from Black applicants, 2018–2024"
    caption="Expected denials are the sum of the white denial rate in each application's own cell of purpose, income, debt-to-income and loan-to-value. The same arithmetic applied to white applications returns exactly the number observed, which is the check that it is arithmetic."
    columns={['', 'Denials']}
    rows={composition.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="composition" set:html={JSON.stringify(compositionSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    That is a ratio of 1.68 and nine standard deviations from the null. Inside those cells the
    unadjusted rates are 23.4 per cent against 12.2; reweighting the white rate to the Black
    applicants' own mix moves it to 13.9, which closes fifteen per cent of the gap.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={gap} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="gap"
    title="Denial rates by applicant race, within income bands, 2023"
    caption="Applications acted on, by the race the register derives from the applicant's own file. 598 of the county's 3,294 rows record no race at all and are in none of these bars."
    columns={['Applicants', 'Per cent denied']}
    rows={gap.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="gap" set:html={JSON.stringify(gapSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Black applicants earning between $50,000 and $99,000 were denied more often than white
    applicants earning under $50,000. That is what the file says, and it is not a finding about
    how anyone was treated: the register carries no credit score, no assets, no reserves and no
    appraisal, and credit history is the leading denial reason in every group here. This site
    publishes the outcome and the missing variables together, and the corpus has an open question
    that says what would settle it — the file's own unread columns first, then six more years of
    it, and after that a record no member of the public can see.
  </p>
</Article>
